<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What Can I Learn From Other Cultures? – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 10px;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring Our Global Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Can I Learn From Other Cultures?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe experiences from a cultural exchange. Interactive: Conduct interviews on general interest topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe cultural exchange experiences, noting specific learning points (e.g., "On my cultural exchange, I adapted to new customs, like greeting with a kiss on the cheek and respecting local food traditions.")
Can conduct interviews to explore cultural interests and perspectives about traveling and learning from other cultures (e.g., "If you could travel anywhere in the future, where would you go?" "If I traveled to Paris, I would explore the history and architecture." "If you visited another country, which one would you choose?" "If I visited Japan, I would experience the cherry blossom festivals, and if I went to the USA, I would explore historical landmarks like the Smithsonian museums in Washington D.C.")
Can respond to interview questions with thoughtful reflections (e.g., "How was your trip abroad influencing your understanding of different cultures and global challenges?" "During the trip, I was noticing cultural differences and learning about global issues, especially how climate change was affecting communities worldwide.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images featuring diverse cultural traditions, famous global destinations (e.g., pyramids, waterfall, historical landmarks), and people engaging in cultural exchanges. Students are asked to verbally identify what they see and share initial thoughts. The teacher asks, "If you could travel to one of these destinations, where would you go and why?" and "What is one unique tradition you can observe in these pictures?" This activates prior knowledge related to 'travel,' 'culture,' 'diversity,' and 'traditions'.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models answering the warm-up questions, integrating target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., "If I could choose, I would visit Egypt because I think the pyramids are the most magnificent ancient wonders. If I traveled there, I would explore their rich history and learn about their unique culture." The teacher then explicitly models the pronunciation of modal verbs with vowel reduction ("could" /kəd/, "would" /wəd/) and stresses multisyllabic words like "adVENture," "des-ti-NA-tion," and "PYR-a-mids" while narrating a hypothetical experience. The teacher also demonstrates respectful discussion of cultural differences, e.g., "What is one tradition you found interesting while you were traveling?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the second conditional and key vocabulary.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say, 'If I could travel, I would visit Japan,' am I talking about a real trip or a dream trip?" (Expected answer: A dream trip/hypothetical.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone describes an 'unforgettable experience,' does that mean they enjoyed it and will remember it, or they disliked it and want to forget it?" (Expected answer: They enjoyed it and will remember it.)</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community")</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a dialogue (approx. 1.5-2 minutes) between two friends discussing their cultural exchange experiences. The dialogue features a student who recently participated in a "year-long Exchange Program" describing their "breathtaking" experiences in a "remote" or "distant" destination and another student expressing their dreams for future travel. The dialogue naturally integrates the second conditional, past continuous, and superlatives, along with vocabulary like 'adventure,' 'destination,' 'understanding,' 'perspectives,' 'benefits,' and 'traditions'.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic competence – understanding and discussing cultural diversity and the personal benefits of travel.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Speaking focus):</b> The teacher plays the audio twice.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> After the first listen, students discuss in pairs: "What were the friends mainly talking about?" and "What did one friend experience, and what does the other dream about?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Sharing:</b> After the second listen, students orally answer questions such as: "What was the speaker doing when they first encountered a new custom?", "According to the dialogue, what is the most enriching aspect of a cultural exchange?", and "If the second friend could travel anywhere, where would they go and why?" (Students justify their answers based on the dialogue).</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice speaking using the second conditional for hypothetical travel, superlatives for comparisons, and past continuous for narrating past experiences. Vocabulary includes 'explore,' 'experience,' 'learn,' 'enjoy,' 'discover,' 'share,' 'breathtaking,' 'enriching,' 'unforgettable,' 'diversity,' 'perspectives.' Focus on correct pronunciation of vowel reduction in "could/would" and stress on multisyllabic words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion & Oral Transformation (Individual to Pair):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems on the board or projector, requiring students to complete them orally using the target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., "If I (can) ______ anywhere, I (would) ______ (visit/explore) ______ because it's the (most beautiful/exciting) ______ destination." "While I (was/were) ______ (travel/explore), I (was) ______ (learn/experience) ______." Students complete them individually and then orally share their sentences with a partner, receiving peer feedback on grammar and vocabulary use.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice: Picture-Prompted Narration (Pair Work):</b> The teacher displays a series of diverse cultural pictures (e.g., a traditional festival, a busy market, people hiking in mountains, a group of students in an Exchange Program). In pairs, students take turns describing what they "would do if they visited this place" (second conditional) and imagining what "they were doing or experiencing if they were there" (past continuous), integrating relevant vocabulary. The teacher circulates, providing support for pronunciation and grammatical accuracy. Example prompt: "Imagine you're at this festival. What would you do? What were people doing when you arrived?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Dialogue Building (Small Groups):</b> In groups of three, students collectively create a short dialogue (4-6 exchanges) about a hypothetical cultural exchange experience, incorporating at least two instances of the second conditional, one superlative, and one past continuous phrase. They orally rehearse their dialogues, focusing on natural flow and accurate pronunciation. The teacher encourages them to use vocabulary like 'unforgettable,' 'enriching,' 'adapt,' and 'broaden perspectives.'</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring Our Global Community")</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Cultural Exchange Interview" - a collaborative interview activity conducted in pairs. One student acts as an interviewer and the other as an interviewee. Then they swap roles.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students engage in a structured interview about real or imagined cultural exchange experiences, connecting directly to the theme "What Can I Learn From Other Cultures?" and the scenario "Exploring Our Global Community."
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interviewer Role:</b> Asks questions related to cultural experiences and hypothetical travel. Example questions: "If you could travel anywhere in the future, where would you go and why?", "How was your trip abroad influencing your understanding of different cultures and global challenges?" and "What is one tradition you found interesting while you were traveling?" (Pragmatic function: respectfully discussing cultural differences and showing interest).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interviewee Role:</b> Describes experiences from a cultural exchange (real or imagined), noting specific learning points. Responds to questions with thoughtful reflections, using the second conditional for hypothetical situations ("If I traveled to Paris, I would explore the history and architecture"), past continuous for narration ("During the trip, I was noticing cultural differences and learning about global issues"), and superlatives for comparisons ("It was the most enriching experience"). They should incorporate vocabulary like 'adventure,' 'diversity,' 'understanding,' 'perspectives,' 'benefits,' 'unforgettable,' and 'traditional.'</li>
                </ul>
                Students should aim to speak for 2-3 minutes each, ensuring a balanced interaction and demonstrating their ability to lead and respond to questions about cultural diversity and travel experiences (Sociolinguistic function: exploring cultural diversity and benefits of travel). The teacher monitors for fluency, interaction, and appropriate use of target language.
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete their "Cultural Exchange Interview." While one pair performs, the other pairs listen and use a simple checklist to provide feedback. The teacher also uses an observation checklist to note student performance.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer & Teacher):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially vowel reduction and multisyllabic word stress).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (nouns, verbs, adjectives related to culture and travel).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of second conditional, superlatives, and past continuous.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of descriptions and responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active listening and showing interest in classmates' stories (pragmatic competence).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative interaction and equal participation.</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an individual reflection card with the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new vocabulary did you use effectively today when describing cultural experiences?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of asking/answering questions about hypothetical travel or past experiences?"</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has discussing cultural exchange experiences today broadened your understanding of other cultures?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one skill you developed today that will help you communicate effectively in a global community?"</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of the speaking activities worked best to engage students and promote fluency? Were students able to consistently use the second conditional, past continuous, and superlatives in their descriptions and interviews? What adjustments are needed for pronunciation practice, especially vowel reduction in modal verbs and stress on multisyllabic words? How can I further scaffold opportunities for respectful discussion of cultural differences for the next lesson?</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson's focus on describing experiences and conducting interviews will serve as a strong foundation for Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), where students will transition from oral descriptions to writing reflective paragraphs about cultural exchange experiences, utilizing the same grammar structures and vocabulary. They will apply their ability to narrate and reflect to a written format.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center;">2-3 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>